# Rust / Tauri conventions

- Commands never block: sync `#[tauri::command]` runs on the main thread and freezes
  the UI. Use `async fn` + `tauri::async_runtime::spawn_blocking` for shell-outs/sleeps.
- Return `AppResult<T>`; add a variant to `AppError` instead of `String` errors.
  `Display` text is English (logs only); map the variant to a `code` in
  `AppError::payload` — the frontend localizes it. Native UI text (tray menu)
  picks zh/en via `locale::is_chinese()`.
- No `unwrap()`/`expect()` outside `lib.rs` startup and tests. Ignoring window
  operation results (`let _ = window.hide()`) is fine for best-effort UI actions.
- Every `unsafe` block needs a `// SAFETY:` comment.
- Invoke system binaries by absolute path (`/usr/sbin/lsof`) — GUI apps get a minimal `PATH`.
- Keep parsing pure and unit-tested with captured fixture output.

```rust
// ❌ blocks the main thread
#[tauri::command]
fn list() -> Vec<ListeningPort> { ports::list_listening_ports().unwrap() }

// ✅
#[tauri::command]
pub async fn list_listening_ports() -> AppResult<Vec<ListeningPort>> {
    tauri::async_runtime::spawn_blocking(ports::list_listening_ports)
        .await
        .map_err(|e| AppError::Task(e.to_string()))?
}
```
